ally  state-of-the-art technology and it's  probably the only thing that could have  saved my life I was very lucky to be  taken to that hospital what the Machine  did was my blood was being taken  external to my body and it was serving  his heart and lungs it was oxygenating  my blood and removing the carbon dioxide  while simultaneously warming my blood so  they were able to to bring me back up to  a more stable temperature and make sure  that my body wasn't deprived of oxygen  in the meantime and how long did this  process take Audrey I think by the time  I arrived at the hospital the timeline  is based on my husband's whatsapp  messages to our friends the emergency  services so I think it was about three  hours and ten minutes from when I lost  consciousness and stopped breathing  until I arrived at the hospital and then  it was another three hours at the  hospital more or less I'm not sure  exactly until they were able to restart  my heart using the defibrillator but  they kept me on the machine for I think  about forty or forty four hours in total  just to make sure that I was stable and  that my brain had recovered enough for  them to detach me and let me I mean  talking to you Audrey it's quite  remarkable to hear what you've gone  through just a month ago how are you now  I feel really back to normal it's it's a  month ago today that I came round that I  woke up and my recovery has been much  more rapid than the doctors expected  there aren't that many documented cases  of recovery from this kind of severe  accidental hypothermia and cardiac  arrests at this length and obviously the  technology is really it can proved a lot  and the team that rescued me and the  doctors did  right so I've been able to recover very  quickly I feel almost as good as new  my hands have some problems with nerve  damage there's a very small price to pay  for what could have been so much worse  and on an emotional level how do you  deal with the knowledge of what you've  been through but also your husband  having witnessed at all it's definitely  worse than it is for me I think I didn't  really take on the seriousness of what  I'd been through until I was definitely  until I was out of hospital and able to  read more and ask more questions and I  so I've had time to absorb it slowly and  take on each new piece of information as  it comes you know he's gonna have to  live with the memories of that day and  he didn't know that this technology  existed he thought I was dead and he's  doing okay he's very strong I think he's  going to be all right but it's  definitely a lot and I saw a clip of you  speaking earlier you're not you're not  ruling out a return to the mountains  Audrey I hope not I love them and I  wouldn't like to let this take that away  from me from us okay Audrey Sherman  thanks very much indeed for talking to  us really a medical phenomenon there  Audrey Schumann who was revived after a  six hour cardiac arrest thank you
 

